    description: "The cron full-suite gate has been RED on dev since at least 2026-08-11. Its\nlog is now readable (WI-zavut's log-access half, dev 87f567d202), so for the\nfirst time the failures can be named.\n\nTHE THREE FAILURES, quoted from the fetched log at dev 45280d90:\n\n  tests/test_rct_public_api_pinned.py::test_run_behavior_map_canonical_import_path\n  tests/test_rct_public_api_pinned.py::test_run_behavior_map_critical_params_pinned\n      ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'rich'\n\n  tests/test_smart_test_mypy_gate.py::TestGateFiresForReal::\n      test_smart_test_refuses_to_run_tests_on_a_regression\n      AssertionError: the all-zero baseline did not produce a regression\n      (rc=2); the fixture is not exercising the gate\n\n  =========== 3 failed, 1498 passed, 2 skipped in 10.66s ============\n\nNONE IS A PRODUCT REGRESSION. Both signatures are the CI environment failing\nto provide what the test needs, not the tree being broken — consistent with\nWI-zavut's measurement that the same tree is green locally on every suite.\n\nWHAT THE STEP INSTALLS, read off the same log rather than inferred:\n\n  + pip install --upgrade pip && pip install pytest pytest-xdist pyyaml 'hypothesis~=6.100'\n  Successfully installed execnet hypothesis iniconfig packaging pluggy\n                         pygments pytest pytest-xdist pyyaml sortedcontainers\n\nNo hypergumbo package, no rich, no mypy. The step's own comment states the\nintent: \"The top-level tests/ are subprocess/file/git tests (NO hypergumbo\npackage install), but need pyyaml + hypothesis (a few property/parse tests\ndo).\" So the contract is deliberate and two tests have outgrown it:\ntest_rct_public_api_pinned loads modules BY PATH via importlib, which\nexecutes cli.py's own `from rich.console import Console`; and the mypy-gate\ntest shells out to scripts/check-mypy-ratchet, which needs mypy on PATH and\nreturns its documented infra-failure code 2 without it.\n\nTHE OBVIOUS FIX IS NOT YET JUSTIFIED, and this is why this is filed rather\nthan fixed. Adding `rich` and `mypy~=2.1.0` to that pip line matches the\nexisting precedent exactly (pyyaml and hypothesis are already there for the\nsame reason). But reconstructing that container locally — a clean venv with\nexactly those four installs — fails 8 of the 9 tests in\ntest_rct_public_api_pinned.py with \"No module named 'hypergumbo_core'\", where\nCI failed only 2 and on a different missing module. Checked: the test file is\nbyte-identical at that SHA (same 9 tests) and .woodpecker/full-suite.yml is\nbyte-identical too. So the CI container had hypergumbo_core importable while\nrich was absent, and neither the workflow file nor a from-scratch\nreconstruction explains how.\n\nUntil that is explained, changing the install list is guessing. It might well\nturn the gate green while leaving the actual mechanism — whatever puts\nhypergumbo_core on that container's path — undocumented and free to break\ndifferently later.\n\nFIRST STEP, now cheap and not available before: fetch the log for the\ntest-all-packages step of the same pipeline and compare its environment, and\nre-fetch after the next cron run (schedule 0 1,13 * * *) to see whether the\nfailure set has changed since 08-11."  # a8a6
    fields:  # a8a6
      statement: "The cron full-suite gate must be green on dev, and any failure it reports must be reproducible outside CI."  # a8a6
      root_cause: "The test-agent-infra step installs pytest, pytest-xdist, pyyaml and hypothesis only. Two tests have outgrown that contract: test_rct_public_api_pinned loads modules by path via importlib and so executes cli.py imports (needs rich), and the mypy-gate test shells out to check-mypy-ratchet (needs mypy, else its documented infra-failure exit 2). A local reconstruction of the same install set does NOT reproduce the CI signature, so the container provides something the workflow file does not describe."  # a8a6
- op: discuss  # 0cd1
  at: '2026-08-14T01:15:09Z'  # 0cd1
  by: agent  # 0cd1
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0cd1
  clock: 2  # 0cd1
  nonce: 0cd1  # 0cd1
  message: "DIAGNOSIS ADVANCED — the sibling step's log is now fetched too, and the\ndiscrepancy sharpens into a specific, testable hypothesis.\n\nFACT 1. test-all-packages (same pipeline, same image python:3.11-bookworm)\ninstalls the full set and PASSES: \"Successfully installed ... rich-14.3.4\n... mypy-2.1.0 ... hypergumbo-core-7.0.0 ...\" then 22389 passed.\n\nFACT 2. test-agent-infra installs ONLY pytest / pytest-xdist / pyyaml /\nhypothesis (+ transitive). Neither hypergumbo nor rich nor mypy appears in\nits install output.\n\nFACT 3, and this is the one that rules out the simple readings: in CI, SEVEN\nof the nine tests in test_rct_public_api_pinned.py PASSED. Those seven do a\nplain `from hypergumbo_core.ir import Edge`. So hypergumbo_core WAS\nimportable in that container. Only the two that reach cli.py failed, and on\n'rich'.\n\nSo the container had hypergumbo_core WITHOUT rich — a combination the\nworkflow file cannot produce, since it installs neither and an editable\ninstall of hypergumbo-core would have brought rich with it (rich~=14.3.2 is\na declared dependency of the package, confirmed in its pyproject).\n\nHYPOTHESIS, labelled as such because I cannot see the agent's volume config:\na PERSISTENT site-packages volume shared across pipelines, carrying a STALE\neditable install of hypergumbo_core from a run predating the rich\ndependency. That single mechanism explains all three observations — the\npackage resolves, its newer dependency does not, and the mypy-gate test's\nsubprocess finds no mypy and returns its documented infra-exit 2.\n\nIT ALSO PREDICTS SOMETHING FALSIFIABLE, which is why it is worth writing\ndown rather than just fixing the pip line: if the volume is stale, adding\n`rich` and `mypy` to that step's install list would turn the gate green\nwhile leaving a stale hypergumbo_core shadowing the checkout — so the step\nwould be testing an OLD tree and reporting on the new one. That is a worse\nfailure than the current red, because it is silent.\n\nNEXT STEP for whoever holds agent access: check whether the Woodpecker agent\nmounts a shared/cached volume over the image's site-packages. If yes, the\nfix is to stop shadowing (or to install the package explicitly in the step),\nnot to top up the dependency list. If no, the hypothesis is dead and the\nmechanism is still unexplained — in which case do NOT paper over it either."  # 0cd1
- op: discuss  # e5f3
  at: '2026-08-14T09:43:07Z'  # e5f3
  by: agent  # e5f3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e5f3
  clock: 3  # e5f3
  nonce: e5f3  # e5f3
  message: "PREMISE RE-CHECKED AND FOUR CANDIDATE MECHANISMS RULED OUT, so the next person\ndoes not re-walk them. The stale-volume hypothesis SURVIVES; nothing here\nconfirms it.\n\nPREMISE HOLDS. Read off the test file at the failing SHA rather than inferred\nfrom the log: of the nine tests in tests/test_rct_public_api_pinned.py, seven\nimport `hypergumbo_core.ranking` / `hypergumbo_core.ir` /\n`hypergumbo_core.linkers.registry` and two import `hypergumbo_core.cli` (which\ndoes `from rich.console import Console`). The seven passed and the two failed\non 'rich'. So `hypergumbo_core` WAS importable in a container that had no\n`rich` -- exactly as filed.\n\nRULED OUT (each checked in the tree, not reasoned about):\n\n1. A conftest putting packages/*/src on sys.path. The repo-root conftest.py\n   does no sys.path work at all (it is the ADR-0010 pytest-wrapper self-heal\n   plus an analyzer-registry fixture). There is no tests/conftest.py.\n\n2. pytest ini path config. pyproject's [tool.pytest.ini_options] sets\n   `pythonpath = [\"packages/hypergumbo-tracker/tests\"]` only -- the tracker's\n   shared `helpers` module. No package src dir.\n\n3. A test inserting src itself. Across tests/*.py the only sys.path inserts\n   are test_filter_transcript.py (a script dir), test_pool_utils.py\n   (scripts/lib) and two string literals inside test_transcript_perms.py\n   subprocess bodies. None adds a package src.\n\n4. The Woodpecker `local` backend (which would ignore `image:` and run on a\n   host that already has an editable install). Argues against itself in the\n   logs: BOTH steps show a from-scratch \"Successfully installed ...\" list,\n   which is a clean container, not a warm host.\n\nSTILL UNSEEABLE FROM HERE, and this is the actual blocker: there is no\nWoodpecker agent config, no docker-compose, and no running container on this\nbox (checked ~/woodpecker*, /etc/woodpecker*, `docker ps`, and a filesystem\nsearch for a woodpecker compose file -- all absent). So whether a volume is\nmounted over site-packages cannot be answered from this machine at all.\n\nPROPOSED NEXT STEP that does not require agent access and cannot paper over the\nmechanism: add a PURE DIAGNOSTIC block to the test-agent-infra step -- print\n`sys.executable`, `sys.path`, `hypergumbo_core.__file__` (guarded), `pip list`,\nand the contents of site-packages (looking for a .pth) -- BEFORE pytest runs,\nand change NOTHING about the install list. It answers the question on the next\ncron run (0 1,13 * * *) and, unlike topping up the pip line, cannot turn the\ngate green while a stale package shadows the checkout. `.woodpecker/**` is not\na governance path, so this needs no approval.\n\nNOT DONE HERE: the diagnostic is not added yet, and the log has not been\nre-fetched since 08-14T01:15Z."  # e5f3
